  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14199831/assets/e7c6cb8f0d7746c1d923ed80a9e05ba4f8eeb9ea3ae88c7b08bcd02037d8ec8a.png)\n\nThe two triangles shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠H+m∠J+m∠L & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H+{69}^{∘}+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H+{109}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H & ={71}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠V+m∠W+m∠Y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {71}^{∘}+m∠W+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠W+{111}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠W & ={69}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the triangles are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠H≅∠V,\\,∠J≅∠W,\\,∠L≅∠Y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $H$ corresponds to $V,$\n\n- $J$ corresponds to $W,$\n\n- $L$ corresponds to $Y.$\n\nTherefore, $HJL∼VWY.$",
